#============================================================================

## SECTION 1: SYMBOLS & NOTATION
##
## All JARVIS operations use these primitive symbols:
##
##   ⊙ = operation marker (thing JARVIS does)
##   ↔ = bidirectional (two-way between kernel and frontend)
##   → = flow/direction (data or control)
##   ✓ = success/rendered
##   ✗ = failure/not rendered
##   τ = time reference (timestamp)
##   μ = metric reference (render performance)
##   ℓ = ledger reference (frame logged)
##

---

## SECTION 2: THE PRIMITIVE

One binary choice underlies all JARVIS integration:

   OUTPUT MODE SELECTION:
   ├─ TERMINAL (CLI/ARIAShell, text-only output)
   └─ WEB (HTTP/WebSocket, full UI rendering)

This choice is made ONCE at startup, immutable for session lifetime.

---

## SECTION 3: TIER 1 OPERATIONS — INITIALIZATION & MODE SELECTION (<1ms)

⊙:initialize_output_mode
  Input: mode str (terminal|web), port int (optional, for web)
  Output: mode_selected, server_status (if web) | shell_ready (if terminal)
  Effect: Select one of two mutually-exclusive output paths. Instantiate chosen renderer.
  Time: <1ms (selection only)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, initialization only)
  Reverse Causality: Mode definition exists BEFORE mode is selected
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches system startup pattern (choose display mode early)
    2. Clarity: Mode is one of exactly two (terminal OR web, never both)
    3. Visibility: Mode choice logged to ledger_jarvis_session.jsonl
    4. Kindness: User has explicit choice via --mode argument
    5. Scaling: O(1), independent of any other parameter

⊙:create_kernel_instance
  Input: None
  Output: kernel ARIAKernel instance, ready
  Effect: Instantiate singleton ARIAKernel (shared by both output modes)
  Time: <1ms (constructor only)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1)
  Reverse Causality: Kernel class exists BEFORE instance created
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard object instantiation pattern
    2. Clarity: One kernel instance per session
    3. Visibility: Kernel ready status logged
    4. Kindness: Kernel is accessible to both terminal and web paths
    5. Scaling: O(1), single instance always

---

## SECTION 4: TIER 2 OPERATIONS — FRAME ROUTING DECISION (<10ms)

⊙:route_frame_to_output
  Input: frame RenderFrame (JSON), current_mode str, context dict
  Output: rendered_output str (terminal) | broadcast_sent bool (web), ✓|✗
  Effect: Determine which renderer to use based on mode. Do NOT change modes mid-flight.
  Time: <10ms (routing decision)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached decision)
  Reverse Causality: Output paths (terminal vs web) defined BEFORE frame arrives
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches pipeline routing pattern
    2. Clarity: Frame routed to exactly one path (terminal XOR web)
    3. Visibility: Routing decision recorded for audit
    4. Kindness: No silent drops; routed frame always reaches destination
    5. Scaling: O(1) routing, independent of frame size or node count

⊙:validate_render_frame
  Input: frame RenderFrame, expected_schema dict
  Output: valid ✓|✗, errors [] (if any)
  Effect: Verify frame matches schema (9 node types, valid layout, animatable)
  Time: <10ms (schema validation)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2)
  Reverse Causality: Node type enum and layout types defined in spec BEFORE validation
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard schema validation (JSON Schema pattern)
    2. Clarity: Frame either valid or invalid with specific error list
    3. Visibility: Validation errors logged
    4. Kindness: Clear error messages for debugging
    5. Scaling: O(n) where n = nodes (typically 10-100, bounded)

⊙:select_renderer_engine
  Input: mode str, frame_size int, client_capabilities dict (if web)
  Output: renderer_engine (JarvisShell | JarvisServer), config dict
  Effect: Choose rendering engine and its configuration based on mode and context
  Time: <10ms (selection + config)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2)
  Reverse Causality: Engine types defined BEFORE selection made
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Strategy pattern for renderer selection
    2. Clarity: Exactly one renderer selected
    3. Visibility: Renderer choice logged
    4. Kindness: Renderer matches user's output mode preference
    5. Scaling: O(1) selection

---

## SECTION 5: TIER 3 OPERATIONS — FRAME RENDERING & LEDGER (<50ms, creates ledgers)

⊙:render_frame_terminal
  Input: frame RenderFrame, shell JarvisShell instance
  Output: rendered_output str (ASCII art), render_time_ms float
  Effect: Convert RenderFrame to terminal-compatible text. Print to stdout.
  Time: <50ms (ASCII rendering)
  Ledger: ledger_jarvis_frames.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Terminal node types and ASCII symbols defined BEFORE rendering
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches terminal rendering pattern (printf family)
    2. Clarity: Output is deterministic (same frame → same output always)
    3. Visibility: Every frame render logged to ledger with frame_id, render_time
    4. Kindness: Terminal wraps at window width, handles ANSI colors
    5. Scaling: O(n) where n = total characters, typically <10,000

⊙:render_frame_web
  Input: frame RenderFrame, server JarvisServer instance, socket WebSocket (or None for HTTP)
  Output: sent bool, bytes_sent int, clients_reached int
  Effect: Convert RenderFrame to JSON. Send via WebSocket (or HTTP fallback). Record in ledger.
  Time: <50ms (JSON serialization + network transmission starts)
  Ledger: ledger_jarvis_frames.jsonl (append frame record)
  Reverse Causality: Frame JSON schema and WebSocket protocol defined BEFORE rendering
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches web API pattern (REST + WebSocket)
    2. Clarity: Frame sent to all connected clients identically
    3. Visibility: Every send logged with frame_id, bytes, client count
    4. Kindness: Clients see same frame simultaneously (no partial updates)
    5. Scaling: O(c) where c = connected clients (typically <1000, linear)

⊙:record_frame_render
  Input: frame_id str, output_mode str, destination str, render_time_ms float, bytes_sent int
  Output: ledger_entry dict, written ✓|✗
  Effect: Append frame render record to ledger_jarvis_frames.jsonl. Creates ledger on first call.
  Time: <5ms (ledger append)
  Ledger: ledger_jarvis_frames.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Ledger structure defined BEFORE any frame is recorded
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches append-only audit log pattern
    2. Clarity: One entry per frame, chronological order, immutable
    3. Visibility: Full frame history available for analysis
    4. Kindness: Ledger shows performance (render_time) for debugging
    5. Scaling: O(1) per write, millions of frames supported

⊙:broadcast_frame_to_clients
  Input: frame RenderFrame, server JarvisServer, client_list [] (optional filter)
  Output: broadcast_record {sent_at τ, clients_reached int, bytes_total int}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Send same frame to all connected WebSocket clients (or filtered list). Record.
  Time: <50ms (parallel broadcast)
  Ledger: ledger_jarvis_broadcasts.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Broadcasting protocol defined BEFORE broadcast attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches pub-sub broadcast pattern
    2. Clarity: All clients receive identical frame
    3. Visibility: Broadcast recorded with timestamp and client count
    4. Kindness: If a client is slow, don't block others (async)
    5. Scaling: O(c) parallel, where c = clients

---

## SECTION 6: TIER 4 OPERATIONS — COMPLETE RENDER PIPELINE (<100ms, composition)

⊙:complete_render_cycle
  Input: frame RenderFrame, mode str, context dict (kernel, server/shell, clients)
  Output: render_result {mode, rendered ✓|✗, destination, latency_ms}, record τ
  Effect: [validate frame] → [select renderer] → [render (terminal or web)] → [record to ledger]
  Time: <100ms (4 sub-operations in sequence)
  Ledger: ledger_jarvis_frames.jsonl + ledger_jarvis_broadcasts.jsonl (both from sub-ops)
  Reverse Causality: Complete pipeline defined BEFORE cycle executed
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches render pipeline pattern (validate → select → render → log)
    2. Clarity: Cycle either succeeds or fails with specific reason
    3. Visibility: Every cycle step logged, end-to-end latency recorded
    4. Kindness: If any step fails, ledger shows which step and why
    5. Scaling: O(n+c) where n = nodes, c = clients (both bounded, linear)

⊙:handle_user_input_from_web
  Input: input_event InputEvent (click, input, gesture, widget_change), frame_context dict
  Output: action_result dict, new_frame RenderFrame
  Effect: [validate event] → [route to kernel or multiuser/network] → [get new frame] → [render] → [broadcast]
  Time: <100ms (user interaction latency)
  Ledger: ledger_jarvis_input.jsonl (created on first call, input events), ledger_jarvis_frames.jsonl (new frame)
  Reverse Causality: Input event types defined BEFORE input handling attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches web form input handling
    2. Clarity: One action executed per event
    3. Visibility: Input logged (what user did) before execution
    4. Kindness: User sees result immediately (no lag > 100ms)
    5. Scaling: O(e) where e = event complexity (typically O(1))

⊙:synchronize_multiuser_state_on_render
  Input: frame RenderFrame, multiuser MultiuserCapabilityLibrary, network NetworkCapabilityLibrary
  Output: sync_result {synced bool, users_notified int, conflicts_resolved int}, ✓|✗
  Effect: If frame involves shared resources: [check permissions] → [get presence] → [broadcast event] → [await consensus]
  Time: <100ms (composition of multiuser/network ops)
  Ledger: ledger_jarvis_multiuser_sync.jsonl (created on first call)
  Reverse Causality: Multiuser/network operations exist BEFORE attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches collaborative system pattern
    2. Clarity: Sync either succeeds or specific users missing/conflicted
    3. Visibility: All users informed, all conflicts logged
    4. Kindness: User never loses work (conflicts preserved in ledger)
    5. Scaling: O(u*c) where u = users, c = conflicts (bounded by nature of cooperation)

---

## SECTION 7: CONSISTENCY & AUDIT

⊙:verify_frame_fidelity
  Input: frame RenderFrame, rendered_output (terminal) | JSON (web)
  Output: fidelity_report {frame_id, nodes_rendered int, nodes_total int, content_loss bool}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Verify all nodes from RenderFrame were rendered (no silent omissions)
  Time: <100ms (verify all nodes)
  Ledger: None (read-only verification)
  Reverse Causality: Node rendering rules defined BEFORE verification
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard completeness check
    2. Clarity: Either all nodes rendered or specific nodes missing (list them)
    3. Visibility: Report shows exactly what was rendered
    4. Kindness: Clear if rendering was partial (browser disconnected, memory limit, etc.)
    5. Scaling: O(n) linear, bounded by node count

⊙:audit_render_performance
  Input: filter {mode: str, since τ, until τ, limit int}
  Output: audit_records [] {timestamp, frame_id, mode, render_time_ms, bytes, clients}, count int
  Effect: Query ledger_jarvis_frames.jsonl + ledger_jarvis_broadcasts.jsonl for matching records
  Time: <100ms (filter audit logs)
  Ledger: None (read-only, multiple ledgers queried)
  Reverse Causality: Audit structure defined BEFORE query
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard audit trail pattern
    2. Clarity: Records ordered by timestamp
    3. Visibility: Complete render history available
    4. Kindness: User can identify slow renders, investigate causes
    5. Scaling: O(m) linear scan acceptable for audit

